**Course Overview.** SIMUS introduces students to MATLAB and Simulink as tools for simulating and controlling dynamic systems. Starting from basic block-diagram modeling, the course progresses through state-space representations, integration algorithms, and classical control design, before moving to nonlinear control, observer design, and a comparison of control strategies (PID, pole placement, LQR).

**Topics covered:** MATLAB/Simulink fundamentals (models, solvers, sampling); state-space representations (LTV, LTI, nonlinear, control-affine); transfer functions and open/closed-loop simulation; discretization effects and zero-order hold; state feedback and pole placement (Ackermann's formula); Luenberger observers and observer-controller duality; nonlinear dynamic control (feedback linearization, mobile robot example); PID tuning, LQR design, and comparative performance analysis.

Lecture slides, TD session notes, and the course project statement are attached to each session above.
